Create a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to write a
Python script from scratch that reproduces the following behavior.
The prompt must describe purpose, inputs/outputs, dependencies, key logic, error
handling, edge cases, and usage, without including code or snippets.
Write a Python 3 command-line tool for Android/Termux (shebang pointing to the
Termux Python path) that recursively normalizes file permissions and executable
bits in a directory tree, with optional parallel processing and a progress
display.
Requirements to implement: **Purpose** Walk a target directory tree and enforce
consistent permission modes: directories get a standard directory mode, regular
files get a standard non-executable file mode, and files that should be
executable get an executable mode.
Symbolic links must be left untouched.
The tool reports what it changes and supports a dry-run mode. **Dependencies and
imports** - Use only the standard library, plus an optional progress-bar library
(tqdm).
If tqdm is not installed, fall back to a no-op wrapper that returns the iterable
unchanged and accepts arbitrary keyword arguments. - Optionally import an
external helper module providing a binary-detection function.
If unavailable, fall back to a stub that always returns false, and record
whether the helper is available in a flag. - Use argparse, fnmatch,
multiprocessing, os, stat, sys, time, dataclasses (with field defaults),
pathlib, and typing.
Use `from __future__ import annotations`. **Constants (define as module-level
defaults)** - Directory mode octal 0o775, file mode 0o644, executable mode
0o755. - Executable bit mask composed of owner, group, and other execute bits. -
Default skip directories (frozen set): .git, __pycache__, .idea, node_modules,
.venv, venv, .ruff_cache. - Default executable-directory names (frozen set):
bin, sbin, .bin, libexec, scripts, tools. - Default executable file suffixes
(tuple): .sh, .so. - Default de-executable glob patterns (tuple): *.py. -
Default directory names whose executable children keep exec bits (frozen set):
bin, sbin. **Helper functions** - mode_of(path): return the permission bits
(masked with the permission-mode mask) from stat, or None on OS/permission
errors. - is_symlink(path): return whether the path is a symlink, safely
handling errors by returning false. - Additional helpers should cover: deciding
whether a file should be executable (based on name suffix, parent directory
name, glob patterns, shebang line presence starting with "#!", and binary
detection when available), deciding whether to strip exec bits (e.g., matching
de-exec patterns outside keep-exec parents), and computing the desired mode for
a path. **Data model** Use a dataclass to hold per-run statistics and per-item
results (e.g., counts of directories, files, executables changed, skipped items,
errors, and elapsed time).
Use field(default_factory=...) for mutable defaults. **Traversal** - Recursively
iterate the tree with os.walk or pathlib, pruning any directory whose name is in
the skip set (and any user-supplied extra skip names).
Do not follow symlinks into directories. - Yield each path as an iterator, with
the progress bar wrapping the iteration. **Key logic** - For each path, compute
the desired mode and compare to the current mode; only call chmod when they
differ.
Never chmod symlinks. - In dry-run mode, do not modify anything; only report
what would change. - Handle permission errors per item by counting and reporting
them without aborting the whole run. **Parallelism** - Support an optional
worker count argument.
When greater than one, use a multiprocessing pool to process items in chunks;
when one or less, process sequentially.
Aggregate results from workers into the statistics dataclass. **Command-line
interface (argparse)** - Positional: target directory (default current
directory). - Options: --dry-run flag; --workers integer (default 1); --skip
(repeatable, extra directory names to skip); --exec-suffix (repeatable, extra
executable suffixes); --deexec-pattern (repeatable, glob patterns); --verbose
flag to print each change; --no-progress flag to disable the progress bar. -
Validate that the target exists and is a directory; print an error to stderr and
exit with a nonzero code otherwise. **Output** - Print a summary at the end:
directories and files examined, number of mode changes applied (or would-be
applied in dry-run), skipped counts, error count, and elapsed time in seconds. -
Verbose mode prints each path changed with old and new modes in octal. - Errors
go to stderr. **Execution** - Entry point guarded by `if __name__ ==
"__main__":`, returns an integer exit status from a main() function, and passes
it to sys.exit. - Handle KeyboardInterrupt gracefully by printing a message and
exiting with code 130. **Edge cases** - Empty directories, broken symlinks,
unreadable files, nonexistent target, target that is a file rather than a
directory, files without extensions, hidden files, and files with uppercase
suffixes (match case-insensitively).
Output only the final script-generation prompt as described. ---
